  clock: 2  # e8d2
  nonce: e8d2  # e8d2
  message: "FROM WI-rovun (branch jgstern-agent/fix/rovun, local, not merged), bears on this item.\nWI-rovun gave LanguageDdgSpec two fields this item can reuse: bound_bodies_for (a bound node that is a BINDING holding bodies, not itself a callable) and bound_anchor_kinds (non-callable symbol kinds admitted into analyzer_symbol_index for that language only). Go's package-level var_spec now walks under the analyzer's variable id. It does NOT cover the file pseudo-symbol anchor.\nLive repro on the WI-rovun tree, 2026-10-01 (verify-claims, claim host_secret -> network, body: k := secret read; print(len(k)); network call with an unrelated url -- the shape a declaration REFUTES):\n  python module-level code:            verdict=violated, evidence analysis_method=structural walk_verdict=unavailable, source python:app.py:1-1:file:file\n  go  var _ = register(func() {...}):  verdict=violated, evidence analysis_method=structural walk_verdict=unavailable, source go:main.go:1-1:file:file\nThe same body as a Go function declaration, a Go package-level var literal (after WI-rovun), or a JS object-literal property arrow/function (WI-mufag) reads no violation. So the residual is exactly the file/module anchor, in both languages measured here. Not measured: the other languages INV-bamij anchored (their DDG specs, where present, list no class/module node).\nDesign note: a file pseudo-symbol aggregates EVERY top-level site in the file, so any walk under it would also have to forfeit refutation whenever it holds more than one body or code outside them -- the same gate WI-rovun applies to a multi-literal var (ddg_build._binding_walk_incomplete)."  # e8d2
